## DESCRIPTION
## Find the number of students in clubs based on percents.
## ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Arithmetic)
## DBchapter(Percents)
## DBsection(Applications)
## KEYWORDS('math club','percent')
## Level(2)
## Static(1)
## MO(1)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

do {
    $sci_num  = random(15, 40, 5);
    $math_pct = random(30, 60, 10);
    $math_num = $sci_num * $math_pct / 100;
    # $sci_num = 100*$math_num/$math_pct;
    $sci_pct   = random(30, 60, 10);
    $math_club = 100 / $sci_pct * $math_num;
    } until (int($math_club) == $math_club
        && int($math_num) == $math_num
        && $math_pct != $sci_pct
        && $math_num != $sci_num);

BEGIN_PGML
In a junior high school, [$math_pct]% of the students in the Math Club are in the Science Club and [$sci_pct]% of the students in the Science Club are in the Math Club.  There are [$sci_num] students in the Science Club.

The number of students in the Math Club is [_]{$math_club}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The Science Club has [$sci_num] members.  [$math_pct]% of [$sci_num] is [$math_num], so [$math_num] members of the Science Club are in the Math Club.  That means that [$math_num] members of the Math Club are in the Science Club.  But since [$math_num] is [$sci_pct]% of the membership Math Club, the number of members of the Math Club must
be 

 [```\frac{100}{[$sci_pct]} \times [$math_num]= [$math_club]```].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
